{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "05-definite-integrals.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Definite integrals and sums",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Evaluate definite integrals with the fundamental theorem, compute finite sums, and watch Riemann sums approach an integral.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Fundamental theorem of calculus\n> If $F' = f$ on $[a, b]$, then $\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx = F(b) - F(a)$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`integrate(f, x, a, b)` finds a checked antiderivative, then evaluates it at the bounds. Step through it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x^2, x, 0, 1)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(sin(x), x, 0, pi)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Sums",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`sum(f, k, a, b)` adds $f$ for $k = a, a + 1, \\dots, b$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(k, k, 1, 10)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(k^2, k, 1, 5)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Riemann sums",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Right Riemann sum\n> Cut $[0, 1]$ into $n$ strips of width $\\frac1n$ and stand a rectangle of height $f(\\frac kn)$ on the $k$-th: the area of the rectangles is $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{n} f\\!\\left(\\tfrac kn\\right) \\tfrac1n$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let n = 10",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null,
      "slider": {
        "min": 1,
        "max": 60,
        "step": 1
      },
      "mode": "raw"
    },
    {
      "src": "let R = sum((k/n)^2 / n, k, 1, n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "N(R)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Drag `n`. The sum is an exact fraction for every $n$, and its decimal closes in on $\\int_0^1 x^2\\,dx = \\frac13 \\approx 0.333$ from above: the rectangles stand above the curve.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x, x, 0, 2)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 x\\,dx$.",
      "hints": [
        "An antiderivative is $\\frac{x^2}{2}$; evaluate at $2$ and at $0$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x^2, x, 1, 3)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_1^3 x^2\\,dx$.",
      "hints": [
        "$F(x) = \\frac{x^3}{3}$; the answer is $F(3) - F(1)$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(2k - 1, k, 1, 6)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Add the first six odd numbers, $1 + 3 + 5 + \\dots + 11$.",
      "hints": [
        "Try $1$, then $1 + 3$, then $1 + 3 + 5$: what do the totals have in common?"
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A definite integral is a difference of antiderivative values, and the limit of Riemann sums: the same number reached two ways.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
